{"step_index":176,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-28T17:39:25Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-28T23:09:25+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-28T23:09:26+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/akhil/.gemini/config/plugins/android-cli-plugin/skills/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 274\nTotal Bytes: 14533\nShowing lines 1 to 274\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: android-cli\n3: description: Provides instructions for installing and using the `android` CLI. The `android` command-line tool is a critical tool for Android development and helps you create new Android projects, run Android apps on devices, manage and interact with Android virtual devices (including screenshots and UI inspection), manage Android SDK components, look up official Android documentation, and discover and install official Android skills.\n4: ---\n5: # Android CLI Specialist\n6: \n7: This skill provides instructions for using the `android` CLI tool. The tool includes various commands for creating projects, running applications, interacting with devices, and managing the CLI environment.\n8: \n9: ## Installation\n10: \n11: If the android tool is not in the path, install it. To install run the following command:\n12: Linux: curl -fsSL https://dl.google.com/android/cli/latest/linux_x86_64/install.sh | bash\n13: Mac Arm: curl -fsSL https://dl.google.com/android/cli/latest/darwin_arm64/install.sh | bash\n14: Mac Intel: curl -fsSL https://dl.google.com/android/cli/latest/darwin_x86_64/install.sh | bash\n15: Windows: curl -fsSL https://dl.google.com/android/cli/latest/windows_x86_64/install.cmd -o \"%TEMP%\\i.cmd\" && \"%TEMP%\\i.cmd\"\n16: \n17: ## SDK management\n18: To manage the installation of Android SDKs and tools, use the `sdk` command. For example:\n19: \n20: - `android sdk install <package>[@<version>]...`: Install specific packages. Multiple packages can be specified, separated by spaces. `<version>` defaults to latest. For example: `android sdk install platforms/android-30@2 platforms/android-34`\n21: - `android sdk update [<pkg-name>]`: Update a specific package or all packages to the latest version.\n22: - `android sdk remove <pkg-name>`: Remove a package from the local SDK.\n23: - `android sdk list --all`: List installed and available SDK packages.\n24: \n25: ## Project creation\n26: Create projects from templates using the `create` command.\n27: \n28: For example: `android create empty-activity --name=\"My App\" --output=./my-app`\n29: \n30: ## Interacting with devices\n31: For more information on interacting with running devices, see [here](references/interact.md)\n32: \n33: ## Running journey tests\n34: For more information on running journeys, see [here](references/journeys.md)\n35: \n36: ## Doc searching\n37: The `docs` command searches authoritative, high-quality Android developer documentation in the Android Knowledge Base.\n38: By providing a few keywords, this tool will return high quality articles that contain examples or guidance on how to use Android APIs or libraries.\n39: Use this tool to obtain additional information on how to achieve Android-specific tasks or to know more about Android APIs, surfaces, libraries, or devices.\n40: \n41: Always use this tool to get the most up-to-date information about Android concepts. Typical good use cases are:\n42:   - Finding migration guides for APIs.\n43:   - Finding examples for APIs.\n44:   - Finding up-to-date information about Android APIs.\n45:   - Finding best practices for Android concepts.\n46: \n47: ## Running APKs\n48: Use the `run` command to run Android apps.\n49: \n50: ## Managing emulators\n51: \n52: Manage Android Virtual Devices (AVDs) using the `android emulator` command\n53: \n54: ## Capturing screenshots\n55: \n56: Capture an image of the current screen of a connected Android device and output it to a file using the `android screenshot` command.\n57: \n58: ## Managing skills\n59: \n60: Manage agent skills for Android using the `android skills` command.\n61: \n62: ## Inspecting UI Layouts\n63: \n64: Use the `android layout` command to inspect the UI layout of an Android application. It returns the layout tree of an Android application in JSON format. When debugging UI errors, this is often a much faster approach than taking a screenshot.\n65: \n66: ## Updating the CLI\n67: \n68: Update the Android CLI using the `android update` command.\n69: \n70: # `android help` output\n71: \n72: Usage: android [-hvV] [--sdk=PARAM] [COMMAND]\n73:   -h, --help        Show this help message and exit.\n74:       --sdk=PARAM   Path to the Android SDK\n75:   -v, --verbose     Enable verbose output for troubleshooting\n76:   -V, --version     Print version information and exit.\n77: Commands:\n78:   create    Create a new Android project from available templates. Allows\n79:               specifying project name, output directory, minSdk, and dry-run\n80:               execution.\n81:   describe  Analyzes an Android project to generate descriptive metadata.\n82:   docs      Android documentation commands for searching and fetching developer\n83:               documentation from the official knowledge base.\n84:   emulator  Manage Android Virtual Devices (AVDs). Includes commands to start,\n85:               stop, list, and view details about emulators.\n86:   help      Shows the help of all commands\n87:   info      Print environment information including SDK location, connected\n88:               devices, and configuration variables. Use specific fields to\n89:               narrow the output.\n90:   init      Initializes the environment for the Android CLI. Sets up required\n91:               configurations, directories, and default skills.\n92:   install   Install an Android Application (one or more APKs) to a connected\n93:               device or emulator without activating any components using\n94:               incremental optimizations for faster deployment than adb.\n95:   layout    Returns the layout tree of an application\n96:   run       Build, deploy, and launch an Android application on a connected\n97:               device or emulator.\n98:   screen    Commands to view the device\n99:   sdk       Manage the Android SDK installation. Includes commands to install,\n100:               update, remove, and list available and installed SDK packages.\n101:   skills    Manage Android CLI skills. Includes commands to install, remove,\n102:               list, and search for skills by keyword.\n103:   studio    Android Studio commands\n104:   update    Update the Android CLI to the latest version.\n105: \n106: create\n107:           Usage: android create [-h] [--verbose] [--list] [--minSdk=api]\n108:                                 --name=applicationName [-o=dest-path] [template-name]\n109:           Create a new Android project from available templates. Allows specifying\n110:           project name, output directory, minSdk, and dry-run execution.\n111:                 [template-name]      The template name\n112:             -h, --help               Show this help message and exit.\n113:                 --minSdk=api         The 'minSdk' supported by the application (default\n114:                                        is defined in the template)\n115:                 --name=applicationName\n116:                                      The name of the application (e.g. 'My Application')\n117:             -o, --output=dest-path   The destination project directory path (default is\n118:                                        '.')\n119:                 --verbose            Enables verbose output\n120:                 --list               List all available templates\n121: \n122: describe\n123:           Usage: android describe [-hV] [--project_dir=PARAM]\n124:           Analyzes an Android project to generate descriptive metadata.\n125:           This command identifies and outputs the paths to JSON files that detail the\n126:           project's structure, including build targets and their corresponding output\n127:           artifact locations (e.g., APKs). This information enables other tools and\n128:           commands to locate build artifacts efficiently.\n129:             -h, --help                Show this help message and exit.\n130:                 --project_dir=PARAM   The project directory to describe\n131:             -V, --version             Print version information and exit.\n132: \n133: docs\n134:           Usage: android docs [-h] [COMMAND]\n135:           Android documentation commands for searching and fetching developer\n136:           documentation from the official knowledge base.\n137:             -h, --help   Show this help message and exit.\n138:           Commands:\n139:             search  Search Android documentation. Enclose keywords in quotes.\n140:             fetch   Fetch an Android documentation article from a URL (kb://...)\n141: \n142: emulator\n143:           Usage: android emulator [-h] [COMMAND]\n144:           Manage Android Virtual Devices (AVDs). Includes commands to start, stop, list,\n145:           and view details about emulators.\n146:             -h, --help   Show this help message and exit.\n147:           Commands:\n148:             create  Creates a virtual device\n149:             start   Launches the specified virtual device. This command will return when\n150:                       the emulator is fully started and ready to use.\n151:             stop    Stops the specified virtual device\n152:             list    Lists available virtual devices\n153:             remove  Delete a virtual device\n154: \n155: help\n156:           Usage: android help [COMMAND]\n157:           Shows the help of all commands\n158:                 [COMMAND]   The command to show help for\n159: \n160: info\n161:           Usage: android info <field>\n162:           Print environment information including SDK location, connected devices, and\n163:           configuration variables. Use specific fields to narrow the output.\n164:                 <field>   The specific field to print the value of. If omitted print all.\n165: \n166: init\n167:           Usage: android init\n168:           Initializes the environment for the Android CLI. Sets up required\n169:           configurations, directories, and default skills.\n170: \n171: install\n172:           Usage: android install [-hv] [--use-delta-install] [--device=PARAM]\n173:                                  [--apks=PARAM[,PARAM...]]... [--install-options=PARAM[,\n174:                                  PARAM...]]...\n175:           Install an Android Application (one or more APKs) to a connected device or\n176:           emulator without activating any components using incremental optimizations for\n177:           faster deployment than adb.\n178:                 --apks=PARAM[,PARAM...]\n179:                                       The paths to the APKs\n180:                 --device=PARAM        The device serial number\n181:             -h, --help                Show this help message and exit.\n182:                 --install-options=PARAM[,PARAM...]\n183:                                       Additional options/flags to pass to package manager\n184:                                         install (e.g. -g,-d)\n185:                 --use-delta-install   Use fast delta install (speeds up incremental\n186:                                         updates by transferring only modified code and\n187:                                         resources; default: true)\n188:             -v, --verbose             Enable verbose logging\n189: \n190: layout\n191:           Usage: android layout [-dhp] [--device=PARAM] [-o=PARAM]\n192:           Returns the layout tree of an application\n193:             -d, --diff           Returns a flat list of the layout elements that have\n194:                                    changed since the last invocation of ui-dump\n195:                 --device=PARAM   The device serial number\n196:             -h, --help           Show this help message and exit.\n197:             -o, --output=PARAM   Writes the layout tree to the specified file or\n198:                                    directory. If omitted, prints the tree to standard\n199:                                    output\n200:             -p, --pretty         Pretty-prints the returned JSON\n201: \n202: run\n203:           Usage: android run [-hv] [--debug] [--use-delta-install] [--activity=PARAM]\n204:                              [--device=PARAM] [--type=PARAM] [--apks=PARAM[,PARAM...]]...\n205:                              [--install-options=PARAM[,PARAM...]]...\n206:           Build, deploy, and launch an Android application on a connected device or\n207:           emulator.\n208:                 --activity=PARAM      The activity name\n209:                 --apks=PARAM[,PARAM...]\n210:                                       The paths to the APKs\n211:                 --debug               Run in debug mode\n212:                 --device=PARAM        The device serial number\n213:             -h, --help                Show this help message and exit.\n214:                 --install-options=PARAM[,PARAM...]\n215:                                       Additional options/flags to pass to package manager\n216:                                         install (e.g. -g,-d)\n217:                 --type=PARAM          The component type (ACTIVITY, WATCH_FACE, TILE,\n218:                                         COMPLICATION, DECLARATIVE_WATCH_FACE, WEAR_WIDGET)\n219:                 --use-delta-install   Use fast delta install (speeds up incremental\n220:                                         updates by transferring only modified code and\n221:                                         resources; default: false)\n222:             -v, --verbose             Enable verbose logging\n223: \n224: screen\n225:           Usage: android screen [-h] [COMMAND]\n226:           Commands to view the device\n227:             -h, --help   Show this help message and exit.\n228:           Commands:\n229:             capture  Outputs the device screen to a PNG\n230:             resolve  Target UI elements visually\n231: \n232: sdk\n233:           Usage: android sdk [COMMAND]\n234:           Manage the Android SDK installation. Includes commands to install, update,\n235:           remove, and list available and installed SDK packages.\n236:           Commands:\n237:             install  Install SDK packages\n238:             update   Update one or all packages to the latest version\n239:             remove   Remove packages from the SDK\n240:             list     List installed and available SDK packages\n241: \n242: skills\n243:           Usage: android skills [-h] [COMMAND]\n244:           Manage Android CLI skills. Includes commands to install, remove, list, and\n245:           search for skills by keyword.\n246:             -h, --help   Show this help message and exit.\n247:           Commands:\n248:             add     [id]      Install a specific skill by its ID to your environment\n249:             remove  [id]      Remove an installed skill by its ID\n250:             list              List installed and available skills\n251:             find    [keyword] Search for available skills in the repository matching a\n252:                       keyword\n253: \n254: studio\n255:           Usage: android studio [-h] [COMMAND]\n256:           Android Studio commands\n257:             -h, --help   Show this help message and exit.\n258:           Commands:\n259:             find-declaration        Find declaration of a symbol\n260:             find-usages             Find usages of a symbol\n261:             open-file               Open a file in Android Studio\n262:             check                   Check the status of running Studio instances\n263:             analyze-file            Analyze a file in Android Studio\n264:             render-compose-preview  Render a Compose preview in Android Studio\n265:             version-lookup          Looks up the latest available versions on the\n266:                                       internet of maven artifacts, Android versions, and\n267:                                       more.\n268: \n269: update\n270:           Usage: android update [--url=PARAM]\n271:           Update the Android CLI to the latest version.\n272:                 --url=PARAM   The URL to dow